WEBVTT

NOTE
SYNTHETIC BENCHMARK FIXTURE. Fictional interview for the Verbatim eval set (Ledgerly study).
Participant p6: Jordan Mbeki, Operations Manager, Riverside Youth Arts (nonprofit), 22 employees. Not a real person.

00:00:03.045 --> 00:00:05.082
<v Interviewer>Hi Jordan, how's it going?

00:00:05.595 --> 00:00:07.036
<v Jordan Mbeki>Good, good. Busy.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:15.054
<v Jordan Mbeki>We've got our fall showcase in two weeks so it's, um, a lot of glitter in the office right now. Literally.

00:00:15.774 --> 00:00:17.734
<v Interviewer>What's the fall showcase?

00:00:18.912 --> 00:00:29.124
<v Jordan Mbeki>Every program does a little performance or exhibit. The ceramics kids do a gallery, the music kids do a concert, and the parents cry.

00:00:29.472 --> 00:00:40.727
<v Jordan Mbeki>It's the best night of the year. It's also the most receipts of the year, which is probably why I'm thinking about all this so much.

00:00:41.523 --> 00:00:49.643
<v Interviewer>[laughs] Sounds fun. We'll record this, if that's okay, and talk about how the first month with Ledgerly has gone.

00:00:50.607 --> 00:00:52.063
<v Jordan Mbeki>Yep, that's fine.

00:00:53.165 --> 00:00:57.383
<v Interviewer>Can you tell me about your role and the organization?

00:00:58.047 --> 00:01:05.915
<v Jordan Mbeki>Sure. I'm the operations manager at Riverside Youth Arts. We're a nonprofit, we run after-school art and music programs.

00:01:06.182 --> 00:01:10.628
<v Jordan Mbeki>Twenty-two staff, plus a bunch of teaching artists who are contractors.

00:01:10.868 --> 00:01:17.154
<v Jordan Mbeki>My job is basically everything that isn't teaching. HR-ish stuff, facilities, the vans, and now expenses.

00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:20.558
<v Interviewer>How did Ledgerly come in?

00:01:21.564 --> 00:01:26.509
<v Jordan Mbeki>Our executive director, Monique, wanted to get rid of the reimbursement forms.

00:01:26.769 --> 00:01:38.372
<v Jordan Mbeki>Staff were buying art supplies with their own money and waiting a month to get paid back, which is not okay when you're paying people nonprofit salaries.

00:01:38.554 --> 00:01:45.528
<v Jordan Mbeki>So we got Ledgerly cards for program staff. We went live, I think, four weeks ago tomorrow.

00:01:46.245 --> 00:01:48.827
<v Interviewer>Why Ledgerly and not something else?

00:01:49.915 --> 00:01:57.876
<v Jordan Mbeki>Honestly, a nonprofit discount and a board member who'd used it at his day job. That's about as scientific as it got.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:09.919
<v Jordan Mbeki>We looked at two others, but one was way too expensive and the other one's demo guy talked for an hour without asking us a single question.

00:02:10.435 --> 00:02:12.190
<v Interviewer>How did setup go?

00:02:12.902 --> 00:02:16.955
<v Jordan Mbeki>Okay, so, funny story. Not funny at the time.

00:02:17.099 --> 00:02:26.291
<v Jordan Mbeki>Our board treasurer, Walt, signed the order form because he's the one who approves contracts. And so Ledgerly decided Walt was the main person.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:34.912
<v Jordan Mbeki>All the setup emails — "finish setting up your account," "connect your bank," all of that — went to Walt.

00:02:35.134 --> 00:02:38.588
<v Jordan Mbeki>Walt is seventy-four and checks his email on Sundays.

00:02:39.409 --> 00:02:40.503
<v Interviewer>Oh no.

00:02:41.422 --> 00:02:48.554
<v Jordan Mbeki>[laughs] Yeah. So I'm sitting there as the actual admin with nothing in my inbox, wondering why nothing's happening.

00:02:48.695 --> 00:02:55.748
<v Jordan Mbeki>We lost almost two weeks before Walt forwarded me a stack of them with "Is this you?"

00:02:56.701 --> 00:02:58.870
<v Interviewer>Were there other email issues?

00:02:59.695 --> 00:03:07.579
<v Jordan Mbeki>When I invited the staff, some of the invites went to our general info address instead of to the person.

00:03:07.819 --> 00:03:17.535
<v Jordan Mbeki>I think because when we first set up the account it pulled contacts from our website or something? I'm honestly not sure.

00:03:17.683 --> 00:03:21.258
<v Jordan Mbeki>But three program coordinators never got their invites.

00:03:21.372 --> 00:03:30.967
<v Jordan Mbeki>One of them, Keisha, found hers in the info inbox a week later, under a pile of parent emails about pickup times.

00:03:31.743 --> 00:03:34.073
<v Interviewer>How did that affect things?

00:03:34.613 --> 00:03:42.451
<v Jordan Mbeki>It just made the start feel sloppy. Like, people were excited about the cards, and then half of them couldn't activate.

00:03:42.599 --> 00:03:45.832
<v Jordan Mbeki>The energy kind of went out of it.

00:03:46.816 --> 00:03:51.418
<v Interviewer>Let's talk about now that people are in. How's day to day?

00:03:52.097 --> 00:03:57.837
<v Jordan Mbeki>The spending part is good. People buy paint, they snap the receipt, it's fine.

00:03:57.954 --> 00:03:59.329
<v Jordan Mbeki>The approvals, though.

00:04:00.461 --> 00:04:02.440
<v Interviewer>Tell me about approvals.

00:04:02.964 --> 00:04:09.134
<v Jordan Mbeki>So we're tiny. Monique is the only real manager. Everybody reports to her or to me.

00:04:09.391 --> 00:04:18.592
<v Jordan Mbeki>But Ledgerly wanted us to set up a chain, like, a first approver and a second approver, and then finance, and finance is also me.

00:04:18.806 --> 00:04:29.342
<v Jordan Mbeki>I couldn't figure out how to make it just one person. So right now every purchase goes to Monique, then to me, then to me again as finance.

00:04:29.621 --> 00:04:32.099
<v Jordan Mbeki>[laughs] I approve my own approval.

00:04:32.471 --> 00:04:35.342
<v Interviewer>How does that play out in practice?

00:04:35.797 --> 00:04:40.114
<v Jordan Mbeki>Monique travels a lot for fundraising. When she's gone, everything stops.

00:04:40.281 --> 00:04:50.769
<v Jordan Mbeki>A thirty-dollar order of clay sits there for nine days because she's at a conference and the app won't let anyone else step in.

00:04:51.062 --> 00:04:57.344
<v Jordan Mbeki>For a team our size, we need, like, "Jordan or Monique, whoever's around." Not a ladder.

00:04:58.521 --> 00:05:00.506
<v Interviewer>Did you try a workaround?

00:05:01.239 --> 00:05:11.783
<v Jordan Mbeki>I tried making a delegate, but the delegate setting only works if the person is at the same "level," and there isn't anyone at Monique's level.

00:05:11.899 --> 00:05:13.214
<v Jordan Mbeki>It's just her.

00:05:13.969 --> 00:05:17.916
<v Interviewer>What do the program staff think about the waiting?

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:28.851
<v Jordan Mbeki>They're very patient people, they work with eleven-year-olds. But one of our music teachers asked me if she should just go back to the paper form.

00:05:28.953 --> 00:05:31.127
<v Jordan Mbeki>That one stung a little.

00:05:31.509 --> 00:05:34.461
<v Interviewer>How does Monique feel about the approvals?

00:05:35.188 --> 00:05:41.699
<v Jordan Mbeki>She's busy, so she doesn't complain, she just doesn't approve things. Which is worse. [laughs]

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:48.804
<v Jordan Mbeki>She told me she gets so many notifications that she's started ignoring all of them, including the Ledgerly ones.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:54.746
<v Jordan Mbeki>So then I text her. "Can you approve the clay?" It's a very glamorous job.

00:05:55.915 --> 00:05:59.046
<v Interviewer>Okay. And what about the accounting side?

00:05:59.746 --> 00:06:08.241
<v Jordan Mbeki>Ugh. So, we use QuickBooks Online, and for a nonprofit the most important thing is tracking which grant pays for what.

00:06:08.578 --> 00:06:11.058
<v Jordan Mbeki>We use classes for each grant.

00:06:11.349 --> 00:06:16.074
<v Jordan Mbeki>When Ledgerly sends the expenses over, the grant doesn't come with it.

00:06:16.352 --> 00:06:23.001
<v Jordan Mbeki>Everything shows up in QuickBooks with no class, and a lot of it in "Uncategorized Expense."

00:06:23.931 --> 00:06:26.548
<v Interviewer>How do you deal with that?

00:06:27.461 --> 00:06:34.090
<v Jordan Mbeki>Our bookkeeper, Sam, comes in on Thursdays. She's been re-coding every single transaction by hand.

00:06:34.397 --> 00:06:42.712
<v Jordan Mbeki>She said it's actually more work than the paper forms, because at least the forms had the grant name written on them.

00:06:42.964 --> 00:06:53.552
<v Jordan Mbeki>And for our funders, that's a big deal. If I report that the arts council grant paid for something it didn't, that's a real problem for us.

00:06:53.928 --> 00:06:55.874
<v Interviewer>Did you contact support?

00:06:56.925 --> 00:07:00.788
<v Jordan Mbeki>I did. They sent me to the mapping page.

00:07:01.128 --> 00:07:05.948
<v Jordan Mbeki>It looks fine on the mapping page! That's the frustrating part.

00:07:06.144 --> 00:07:10.979
<v Jordan Mbeki>On our end, every category has a grant attached. It just doesn't arrive.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.116
<v Interviewer>Does your board look at the spending?

00:07:15.137 --> 00:07:22.711
<v Jordan Mbeki>The finance committee does, once a quarter. Walt, again. [laughs] He likes paper reports, so I print things out for him.

00:07:23.919 --> 00:07:31.698
<v Interviewer>Let me ask a broader question. Outside of Ledgerly, what's going on that affects how people use new tools?

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:36.449
<v Jordan Mbeki>Turnover, honestly. Teaching artists come and go. And everyone's stretched.

00:07:36.548 --> 00:07:41.753
<v Jordan Mbeki>We're doing a gala in the spring and I'm already getting emails about centerpieces.

00:07:41.899 --> 00:07:50.357
<v Jordan Mbeki>Oh, and we're painting a mural on the side of the building with the teens. That's been the fun part of my month.

00:07:51.475 --> 00:07:53.908
<v Interviewer>That sounds great. What's the mural?

00:07:54.346 --> 00:07:59.525
<v Jordan Mbeki>A river, obviously. [laughs] With musical notes in it. The kids designed it.

00:08:00.251 --> 00:08:03.825
<v Interviewer>Love that. Do the teaching artists get cards too?

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:10.870
<v Jordan Mbeki>Not yet. They're contractors, so we're still figuring out whether that's allowed with our insurance.

00:08:11.026 --> 00:08:21.631
<v Jordan Mbeki>For now they buy things and I pay them back the old way, which is a little awkward next to staff who have shiny new cards.

00:08:22.035 --> 00:08:26.606
<v Interviewer>How do you handle the vans? Does that go through Ledgerly?

00:08:27.315 --> 00:08:40.613
<v Jordan Mbeki>Gas and the car wash go on my card. The vans are older than some of our staff, so there's also a lot of "the van is making a noise," which is not a Ledgerly problem.

00:08:40.865 --> 00:08:49.387
<v Jordan Mbeki>Our maintenance guy, Eddie, has a card now, and he's thrilled. He says it's the first time anyone's trusted him with plastic.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:55.202
<v Interviewer>If a new nonprofit asked you whether to use Ledgerly, what would you say?

00:08:55.745 --> 00:09:05.463
<v Jordan Mbeki>I'd say the cards are great, the staff love not waiting for their money. But go in knowing the setup will take longer than they tell you.

00:09:05.759 --> 00:09:11.210
<v Jordan Mbeki>And have your bookkeeper look at it before you sign. Not after.

00:09:12.104 --> 00:09:15.899
<v Interviewer>Back to Ledgerly — if you could change one thing?

00:09:17.042 --> 00:09:22.652
<v Jordan Mbeki>Approvals. Let me say "one approver, and if they're out, the backup." That's it.

00:09:22.979 --> 00:09:25.534
<v Jordan Mbeki>That'd fix most of my week.

00:09:25.736 --> 00:09:30.226
<v Jordan Mbeki>And make the grant actually show up in QuickBooks. Those two.

00:09:31.248 --> 00:09:33.117
<v Interviewer>That's clear. Anything else?

00:09:34.141 --> 00:09:39.917
<v Jordan Mbeki>Just — check who the emails are going to when someone signs up. Ask.

00:09:40.026 --> 00:09:43.820
<v Jordan Mbeki>"Who's running this?" Walt didn't need all those emails.

00:09:44.783 --> 00:09:47.346
<v Interviewer>[laughs] Noted. Thanks so much, Jordan.

00:09:48.025 --> 00:09:50.264
<v Jordan Mbeki>Thanks! Back to the glitter.
